  "kind": "build",
  "title": "# AI keys: store every key in the console, not per user Read CLAUDE.md and the rules first.",
  "asked": "# AI keys: store every key in the console, not per user Read CLAUDE.md and the rules first. One agent, no sub-agents. First check PR #238 (claude/claude-accounts-switch). If it is not merged into main, merge origin/claude/claude-accounts-switch into this branch before you start, so you build on the AI keys plugin with Manage Claude accounts and switch.mjs. ## Problem Brett saved 4 Claude subscription accounts and 1 API key in AI keys while logged into the console as himself. The VPS side (apps/console-web/plugins/ai-keys/switch.mjs, deploy/claude-headless.sh, the /api/plugins/ai-keys routes called with AXF_TOKEN from /opt/axf/.axf-console/env) authenticates as a different console user, and GET /keys returns NO keys. So the VPS can't see or switch accounts. ## Do 1. Make the AI keys store console-wide: one store for the whole console, the same keys, active choice, priority order and VPS state no matter which user or token calls the API. Do not tie keys to the caller's user anywhere (store, listing, active, order, switch, vps sync, credit switch). 2. Only console admins (and the console's own AXF_TOKEN service identity) may read the listing or change anything. Use the console's existing role/permission checks. Never return tokens, only hints, as today. 3. Migration on startup: if any per-user stores exist, merge every key into the console-wide store (no duplicates; keep labels, emails, pairs, last tests; keep the existing active account if there is one). Keep the old files as a .bak, never delete them. Log what was merged without secrets. 4. Tests in plugins/ai-keys/test.py: two different users and the service token see the same keys; non-admin refused; migration merges and dedupes; the switch works when called with the service token. 5. Update docs section 8 in install_instructions.md: keys are stored once in the console. 6. Run the ai-keys test, console tsc, check-theme, check-controls, plugin-lifecycle, menu-reach and help-coverage --check. All must pass. Never skip tests. 7. Open a PR, auto-merge when green. After merge, redeploy the axf-console container so it's live. 8. Verify on the VPS: with AXF_TOKEN from /opt/axf/.axf-console/env, GET https://console.amberxai.com/api/plugins/ai-keys/keys lists Brett's 5 keys (print names and emails only, never tokens). Phone-readable summary at the end: PR link, what changed, verify result. Watch live: Brett can watch the screen you are changing hot-render in the console. Whenever you start work on a screen or element, run `node framework/cli/axf.mjs focus <app> [route] [css-selector] --note \"<what you are changing>\"` (apps: console-web, axhub-web, amberx-droid, walkthrough-studio, demo, framework, console, axhub-android, voicevault). Agent cap: heavy steps (pnpm/npm/vite builds, docker node:22 test runs, Playwright) share a VPS-wide lock. Run each one as `node /opt/axf/deploy/../framework/cli/agent-slots.mjs heavy -- <command>` so only a few run at once. Model switches: a downgrade (e.g. Fable → Opus) is fine. Fable upgrades need no approval right now (Brett, Nov 4 2026: \"If it needs Fable, set it to be Fable\"): a step that needs Fable (schemas, asset types, contracts, engines/kernel, cross-app protocols) is set to Fable and runs on Fable; the switch is recorded on the build. Never launch a step on a higher model than it is pinned to: raise the step first.",
